Distributed Systems 2026/27 - Practical Assignment
===================================================

Plain Java (JDK 11 or newer), no external dependencies.

Layout
------
  src/ds/assignment/tring   Exercise 1 - mutual exclusion with a token ring
  src/ds/assignment/p2p     Exercise 2 - anti-entropy in a P2P network
  src/ds/assignment/tom     Exercise 3 - totally-ordered multicast
  scripts/                  build and run scripts

Compiling
---------
  $ ./scripts/build.sh

The classes are written to build/.

Exercise 1 - Token Ring
-----------------------
Quick demo on a single machine (server + 5 peers on different ports):

  $ ./scripts/tring-local.sh

On 6 machines (m1..m5 for the peers, m6 for the server), after compiling on each:

  m6$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Server
  m2$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m3 IP-of-m6
  m3$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m4 IP-of-m6
  m4$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m5 IP-of-m6
  m5$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m1 IP-of-m6
  m1$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer IP-of-m2 IP-of-m6 --token

Exactly one peer is started with --token: it creates the token and puts it in
circulation. Start it last, or in any order: a peer keeps retrying until the
next one is reachable.

Peer options:
  --port N      port where this peer waits for the token (default 5000)
  --name NAME   label used in the output (default: the host name)
  --token       this peer creates the token

The server listens on port 6000 by default; pass another port as its only
argument. Hosts may be given as host:port when the defaults are not used.

Fault tolerance (extra marks)
-----------------------------
The token keeps moving when a peer fails:

  * Skipping a dead peer. A peer that receives the token replies with
    "ACK <address of its own next peer>". The sender therefore learns who comes
    two positions ahead without any extra configuration. If the next peer does
    not acknowledge the token, it is sent to that second peer instead. The
    original next peer is tried first on every round, so a peer that is
    restarted rejoins the ring by itself.

  * Regenerating a lost token. If a peer dies while holding the token, the
    others stop seeing it. A peer that sees no token for 20 to 30 seconds (the
    exact value is random per peer) creates a new one. The token carries a
    generation number (epoch) that is incremented on regeneration, and a peer
    discards any token with an epoch lower than the highest it has seen, so an
    old token that reappears does not circulate together with the new one.

To try it with the local demo running, from another terminal:

  $ pkill -f "name p3"        # the ring continues with 4 peers
  $ java -cp build ds.assignment.tring.Peer localhost:5004 localhost:6000 \
        --port 5003 --name p3                       # p3 rejoins

  * Two regenerated tokens. If two peers regenerate the token at about the same
    time, both new tokens have the same epoch. The token therefore also carries
    the name of the peer that created it, and tokens are compared by (epoch,
    creator name): a peer discards a token lower than the highest it has seen.
    The lower token is dropped at the first peer that has already seen the
    higher one, so only one survives. (Peer names must be unique.) To see it,
    start two peers with --token: one of the tokens is discarded within a round.

Limitation: two consecutive dead peers stop the ring, because only one backup
address is known.

Exercise 2 - Anti-Entropy
-------------------------
Quick demo on a single machine (6 peers on different ports, with the topology
of the figure: p1-p2, p2-p3, p2-p4, p4-p5, p4-p6):

  $ ./scripts/p2p-local.sh          # every peer starts with a random value
  $ ./scripts/p2p-local.sh one      # p1 starts with 1, the others with 0

On 6 machines, after compiling on each:

  m4$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer
  m2$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer IP-of-m4
  m1$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer IP-of-m2
  m3$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer IP-of-m2
  m5$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer IP-of-m4
  m6$ java -cp build ds.assignment.p2p.Peer IP-of-m4

A peer registers itself with the peers given as arguments, and both sides add
each other to their maps, so each link of the figure is given only once. The
peers can be started in any order: a peer keeps retrying until its neighbour is
reachable.

Peer options:
  --port N      port where this peer waits for connections (default 7000)
  --name NAME   label used in the output (default: the host name)
  --value V     initial value (default: random in (0, 1))
  --rate R      synchronizations per minute (default 2)

Twice per minute on average (Poisson process), a peer picks a random neighbour
and sends it "SYNC <value>". The neighbour moves to the average of the two
values and replies with the value it had. The first peer then applies the
opposite change to its own value instead of overwriting it, so the sum of all
values is preserved even when a third peer synchronizes with it in between.
All values therefore converge to the average of the initial ones.

To see the network size being estimated, add "--value 0" to every peer except
one, which gets "--value 1": the values converge to 1/6.

Convergence time (extra marks)
------------------------------
  $ ./scripts/p2p-convergence.py                    # sizes 4 8 16 24 32 48 64
  $ ./scripts/p2p-convergence.py 5 10 20 --runs 3   # chosen sizes
  $ ./scripts/p2p-plot.py                           # needs Python's matplotlib

For each size N, the first script starts N peers as local processes, with one
peer set to 1 and the others to 0, and measures how long they take to converge.
Each simulation uses a new random topology: every peer is linked to a random
peer started before it, which gives a random tree and therefore a connected
network, and then N/2 random links are added. The results are appended to
results/p2p-convergence.csv, and the second script plots them into
results/p2p-convergence.png.

  * Convergence: the script follows the value of each peer in its output, and
    the network has converged when every peer is within 1% of 1/N (--tolerance).
    The clock starts when the last link of the network is registered.

  * Rate: at 2 synchronizations per minute a simulation with 64 peers takes about an
    hour, so the simulations run at 300 per minute (--rate). A message between
    local peers takes about a millisecond, so the convergence time only depends
    on how often the peers synchronize; the plot scales the measured times to
    what they would be at 2 per minute.

  * Each size is simulated 5 times (--runs); the plot shows every simulation
    and the mean for each size.

Exercise 3 - Totally-Ordered Multicast
--------------------------------------
Quick demo on a single machine (6 peers on different ports, each one knowing
all the others):

  $ ./scripts/tom-local.sh          # 6 peers, 1 random word per second each
  $ ./scripts/tom-check.sh          # afterwards: did every peer print the same order?

Each peer also writes the words it delivers to logs/tom-pN.txt, which is what
tom-check.sh compares.

On 6 machines, after compiling on each (every peer gets the IPs of the other 5):

  m1$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tom.Peer IP-of-m2 IP-of-m3 IP-of-m4 IP-of-m5 IP-of-m6 --name p1
  m2$ java -cp build ds.assignment.tom.Peer IP-of-m1 IP-of-m3 IP-of-m4 IP-of-m5 IP-of-m6 --name p2
  ... and likewise on m3..m6.

Run it from the project directory, so that resources/words.txt (the dictionary
from which words are chosen) is found. Peers can be started in any order:
messages to a peer that is not up yet are retried until it is.

Peer options:
  --port N      port where this peer listens (default 8000)
  --name NAME   peer identifier, must be unique (default: the host name)
  --rate R      words per minute (default 60, i.e. 1 per second)

How it works:

  * Lamport clock. A peer increments its clock before sending a message and
    stamps the message with it; on receiving a message with timestamp t it sets
    its clock to max(clock, t) + 1.

  * Multicast. Every 1 s on average (Poisson process) a peer picks a random word
    and sends "MSG <timestamp> <sender> <id> <word>" to all other peers, and
    puts the message in its own queue.

  * Acknowledgements. A peer that receives a message puts it in its queue and
    sends "ACK <sender> <id> <me>" to ALL peers.

  * Delivery. The queue is ordered by (timestamp, sender name), which is a total
    order. A message is delivered (its word printed) only when it is at the
    head of the queue and has been acknowledged by every peer. Messages to each
    peer are sent by a single thread, in order (FIFO channels), so once every
    peer has acknowledged a message, no message with a smaller timestamp can
    still arrive. Every peer therefore prints the same sequence of words.

Malicious peers (extra marks)
-----------------------------
Every peer checks the timestamps it receives from each sender:

  (a) Rewriting history: since channels are FIFO and clocks only grow, the
      timestamps of an honest sender are strictly increasing. A message that
      reuses a timestamp already seen from that sender, or is lower than one,
      is rejected.

  (b) Writing in the future: a timestamp more than 100 ahead of the receiver's
      own clock cannot come from an honest peer (all clocks advance together
      with the messages), so it is rejected.

A peer caught cheating is blacklisted: all its later messages are rejected and
are never delivered, so it cannot change the order seen by the honest peers.

To see it, make p6 cheat from its 4th message on:

  $ ./scripts/tom-local.sh past     # p6 reuses an old timestamp
  $ ./scripts/tom-local.sh future   # p6 jumps 1000 ahead

(the option behind this is --evil past|future). The other peers log
"MALICIOUS BEHAVIOR DETECTED" and keep delivering the same sequence; p6 itself
blocks, since its own message is never acknowledged.
